WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
Hi! Ms. Coffee Bean and I would&nbsp;
like to discuss something with you:

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.360
Natural Language Understanding Benchmarking, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.760
NLU Benchmarking in short.&nbsp;
Aaah, why should we care?

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.880
You can have the best machine&nbsp;
learning algorithm in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.800
but if your data is crap, your&nbsp;
model won’t be much better.
 &nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.200
And since our goal is to achieve natural&nbsp;
language understanding – is it though?

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.280
Yes, of course it is, how else should my&nbsp;
smart fridge ever truly understand me?

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:35.120
So because we want to achieve NLU, we also need&nbsp;
ways to measure how far we are to getting there.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.320
But according to this position&nbsp;
paper, NLU benchmarking is broken.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:46.320
So in this video, we are going to discuss:&nbsp;
How NLU benchmarking is conducted nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.640
why it is broken and what this&nbsp;
paper proposes to do about this.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.600
Of course, all this is sprinkled with a little&nbsp;
bit of Ms. Coffee Bean’s opinion here and there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.560
We will try to make it clear where it is the case.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.840
NLU Benchmarking. Why is this even a thing?&nbsp;
Don’t laugh, but the way research measures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.400
progress towards machine learning systems&nbsp;
that can understand natural language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.120
is through tests and benchmarks.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.480
It’s school all over again, but now&nbsp;
the ML models are writing the test.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.360
I don’t know why this procedure is&nbsp;
so hilarious to Ms. Coffee Bean.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.640
Come on, Ms. Coffee Bean, how&nbsp;
else should we go on about it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.640
We need to measure, to quantify,&nbsp;
and write all of it down.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.320
I mean, imagine a tree in a forest growing&nbsp;
so big, that it achieved NLU. Did it even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.960
achieve NLU if there was no scientist to&nbsp;
measure it and write a paper about it?

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.240
Of course not, so here we&nbsp;
are, at the starting point&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.440
of this paper: at tests and benchmarks containing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.280
a lot of examples that a model&nbsp;
should get right to achieve NLU.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.080
Collections of data examples, or in&nbsp;
short: benchmark datasets, can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.160
something like SuperGLUE, containing data from&nbsp;
various tasks that challenge these models.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.680
To give you one example,&nbsp;
let’s take the Word-in-Context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.960
subpart of SuperGLUE probing&nbsp;
for Word Sense Disambiguation.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:15.120
Here the model reads in two sentences and must&nbsp;
decide whether a word used in both sentences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:22.080
has the same sense or not. In this example, bed&nbsp;
has a different meaning in the two sentences.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.360
Of course, there are many other tasks, like&nbsp;
reading comprehension, which is also probed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.760
by the SQuAD 2 English reading comprehension&nbsp;
leader board if you have heard of it.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.960
Anyway, the point here is that guess what:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:41.120
biased models, that are not at all at&nbsp;
NLU-level, are able to solve these benchmarks.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:47.040
Now… we won’t get into the philosophical&nbsp;
rabbit hole of asking who decides that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:54.000
systems are biased and are not at the level of NLU&nbsp;
(although they solve the examples probing for NLU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.120
even better than humans), because it is that&nbsp;
sensitive area where goal posts are either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.880
moved a lot, or people themselves have different&nbsp;
opinions on where the goal post even is.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.160
But let’s formulate the general consensus&nbsp;
like this: Although the models solve these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.840
NLU benchmarks in particular, they do&nbsp;
not seem capable of NLU in general.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.720
They possess the biases specifically required&nbsp;
for these benchmarks, but not for other things.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.160
In other words, these models don’t solve the&nbsp;
task, they only solve the datasets. Taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.880
from another angle, they solve the examples&nbsp;
for the wrong reasons. Bean: What are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.080
right reasons? Ms. Coffee Bean, quiet! That’s…&nbsp;
complicated. Maybe something for another video.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.520
Anyway, to counter the&nbsp;
problems of usual benchmarks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.560
where models are exploiting dataset biases&nbsp;
and spurious correlations left in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.920
like when an answer is correlated with a&nbsp;
specific word, benchmarks like DynaBench&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.040
are going away from the classical IID&nbsp;
benchmark and goes into adversarial mode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.600
by asking human annotators to try to fool neural&nbsp;
models, or at least a reference neural model.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.040
But as the authors of this paper highlight,&nbsp;
adversarially constructed benchmarks will produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.720
challenging examples, I mean: they are built&nbsp;
specifically such that models are set up to fail.&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.120
But they also argue that, this again,&nbsp;
doesn’t help to produce a better way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.800
of benchmarking NLU, because these example&nbsp;
can be circumvented by producing models that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.320
drop one set of the bad reasons it used to&nbsp;
solve the task without “understanding” it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.600
and just choose another set of bad reasons that&nbsp;
are not targeted by the adversarial benchmark.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.480
So the point here is, that usual&nbsp;
benchmarks are broken because of biases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.440
that adversarial benchmarks do not solve&nbsp;
the problem either, so the authors propose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.880
four new criteria that benchmarks should satisfy.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.120
Let’s see what these four criteria are.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.480
We cite: “benchmarks should offer a valid test&nbsp;
of the full set of relevant language phenomena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.120
[Oh god, what does this even mean?], they should&nbsp;
be built around consistently-labeled data [Ms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:06.080
Coffee Bean, explain!], they should offer&nbsp;
adequate statistical power [I don't get it.],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.240
and they should disincentivize the use of&nbsp;
systems with potentially harmful biases.”&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.680
Oh god, let’s take it slow and&nbsp;
see how the authors break up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.400
each of these points into digestible pieces.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:22.000
The first criterion is the validity of&nbsp;
the test. This means that if system A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.000
scores higher than the system B, then A should&nbsp;
be better at the task it was tested it at.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.080
Of course, this criterion will be violated by&nbsp;
a system B exploiting biases in the dataset.&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.080
The authors go on arguing how naturally-occurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.800
examples – think about data samples scraped&nbsp;
from the internet –, are not guaranteeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.240
validity since natural distributions reflect&nbsp;
the biases in the wild, but they also won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.360
necessarily tickle out the relevant abilities&nbsp;
for language comprehension from the model,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:59.120
because answers can be achieved also through&nbsp;
factual knowledge rather than understanding.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.680
Just imagine a reading comprehension task where&nbsp;
the model must extract who the president of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:09.680
United States is from a text. Well, it does&nbsp;
not need to if it has memorized this fact.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.520
Okay, Ms. Coffee Bean… But surely examples&nbsp;
generated by experts should do the trick, right?

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:24.080
No. What do you mean, no? Well… Expert-authored&nbsp;
examples have problems too: they are expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.280
that’s sure, and experts are humans too&nbsp;
and will introduce their own selection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.800
bias – of phenomena they are studying,&nbsp;
for example. So expert annotation does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.680
not guarantee broad testing&nbsp;
coverage for model abilities either.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.800
Ok… Ms. Coffee Bean, what&nbsp;
else is there in the paper?&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.400
Ahh lastly, crowdsourcing where random&nbsp;
people on the internet construct data.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.240
There is no surprise I guess, that this is broken&nbsp;
too because annotators are conserving energy

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.120
(which is just the physicists&nbsp;
way of saying “lazy”),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.720
therefore are generating repetitive, easy cases.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:05.120
I can speak from own experience here.&nbsp;
I worked with the Visual Dialog dataset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.040
where crowd workers had to create a&nbsp;
dialog given an image and its caption.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.960
By going through the examples, it is clear&nbsp;
how uninspired some annotators were, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:20.880
too many examples just talk about the picture&nbsp;
in general and ask if that is in color or not;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.920
instead of focusing more on&nbsp;
what is happening in the image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.720
which would have been the desired annotation.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.560
Okay, so what would the perfect&nbsp;
test set look like if it were valid?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.840
Well, the authors do not really propose&nbsp;
THE solution, otherwise this wouldn’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:41.600
a position paper, but a paper presenting THE&nbsp;
dataset implementing this solution, right?

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:47.200
But they survey some promising directions where&nbsp;
crowdsourcing is combined with expert annotation;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.360
so where experts are filling in the&nbsp;
gaps that laymen could not fill.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:57.600
Or by offering bonuses to crowd workers when&nbsp;
they avoid overused words and constructions.&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.960
Well, and if everything about the&nbsp;
first criterion isn’t going so well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.040
wait until you see the second&nbsp;
criterion of reliable annotation.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:10.240
How many times have you gone through your data&nbsp;
and saw wrong labels? Well, this is either due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:14.640
to careless mislabelling, or just because&nbsp;
the annotation guidelines were unclear or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.280
underspecified – oh, I know that one –
or annotators themselves cannot agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:20.720
on one single label.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.160
And if the benchmark has such&nbsp;
errors, we can see the effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.720
super-human performance on benchmarks.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:32.320
While one human only tries to solve the task&nbsp;
with his own judgement that might not reflect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:37.440
the judgment of other annotators, models can&nbsp;
pick up the patterns of the most frequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:42.320
judgments and herewith surpass&nbsp;
any single human annotator.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:46.880
The solution to this is careful planning and&nbsp;
testing of the annotation guidelines. Or:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:52.080
not compressing three annotations into one,&nbsp;
but keeping the annotation distribution there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.280
to analyze how this fits with the&nbsp;
prediction distribution of the model. Cool.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:03.280
The third criterion is about a benchmark’s&nbsp;
statistical power. The size of the data should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:07.680
large and discriminative enough, such that&nbsp;
differences between two model’s quality&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.640
can be significantly detected. Fulfilling&nbsp;
this is becoming harder and harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:18.080
as the models get better because models&nbsp;
start solving all easy examples correctly.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:24.320
And when easy examples make up most of the data,&nbsp;
models start to differ only in the long tail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:29.840
containing only few samples, not delivering&nbsp;
statistical significance. The solution?

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:34.720
It’s simple: get more data. But&nbsp;
well, the reality is expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:38.160
everything costs, especially when&nbsp;
talking about more difficult data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.400
points. So we see how this becomes&nbsp;
already a field of compromise.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:45.440
The authors bring up the idea&nbsp;
that maybe we can manage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:50.960
formulate each data acquisition effort as&nbsp;
a game where people by playing, annotate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:56.000
data for free. Isn’t this a cool start-up&nbsp;
idea for anyone ethically okay with this?

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.280
And the final and hardest criterion is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:05.280
disincentivizing biased models. So we know&nbsp;
that benchmarks are usually not bias free:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.560
they are following natural distributions (which&nbsp;
are biased, reflecting the world as-is: biased).

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:16.320
Crowd-sourced text is also biased,&nbsp;
reflecting the annotator’s bias.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:22.160
This is of course not news. But the problem of&nbsp;
having these biases in the benchmarks is, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:28.400
models have the incentive to be biased too: they&nbsp;
aim to beat the benchmark, so they must have these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:33.840
biases too, otherwise they would get examples&nbsp;
wrong when applying political correctness to them.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.880
This is of course a huge Pandora Box of&nbsp;
trouble and asks for further research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:44.560
because there is no one-size-fits-all solution
for mitigating bias. “This is not a compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:48.800
reason to accept the status quo,”&nbsp;
write the authors – point taken.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:54.240
An idea of going on about this is to create,&nbsp;
we cite, “additional expert-constructed test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:59.280
datasets and metrics that each isolate&nbsp;
and measure a specific type of bias”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:03.040
next to a primary task that&nbsp;
the model is evaluated on.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:06.080
For me personally, it was surprising to read that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:12.880
we cite: “Even for the SuperGLUE benchmark, which&nbsp;
requires users to compute test set metrics on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:20.160
DNC Winogender test set in order to reveal&nbsp;
test set results for any other target task,

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:23.200
a large majority of papers&nbsp;
that report test set numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:27.040
omit this metric and decline to&nbsp;
report potentially unflattering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:33.360
bias numbers”. Wow, this depends so&nbsp;
much about the mindset of the community…

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.240
What, you cynical Ms. Coffee&nbsp;
Bean. You’re not surprised, eh?&nbsp;

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:40.640
It’s time to conclude this. What&nbsp;
have we learned from this paper?

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:45.120
That data, the heart and brains&nbsp;
of our models is important, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:50.000
very hard to get it right. Happily, a maturity&nbsp;
in the field of Machine Learning is developing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.400
where we move from just model&nbsp;
quality, to data quality.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:12:00.320
It’s best reflected in HuggingFace, where next to&nbsp;
their model hub, they also work on their datasets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:05.440
hub. And no, they are not at all involved&nbsp;
in us saying this, we are genuinely excited.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:10.080
The sad but also thrilling lesson&nbsp;
from this paper is that clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:13.360
more research is needed on this datasets domain.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:18.960
If you think you are more into data creation and&nbsp;
model fiddling is not for you, go ahead and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.720
cool progress for datasets! It is a cool place&nbsp;
just waiting for more smart people to work in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:30.400
Ms. Coffee Bean hopes that the benchmark&nbsp;
creation field will become as sexy as new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:35.120
model architectures like GANs, transformer&nbsp;
versions, etc. in Machine Learning.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:51.840
Thanks for watching until the end.&nbsp;
Ms. Coffee Bean will let herself out.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:55.482
Okay, bye!

